 "standard_text": "Multiplication and division of decimals by powers of 10, by whole numbers and by decimals, and their use in money and measurement problems (summary of NCERT Ganita Prakash Class 7)",
 "meta_desc": "Class 7 Another Peek Beyond the Point worksheet (Ganita Prakash Part 2, Chapter 4): multiplying and dividing decimals by 10, 100, 1,000, by whole numbers and by decimals. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Count the decimal places in both numbers",
 "key_sub": "To divide by a decimal, multiply both numbers by 10 or 100 first.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>× 10, 100, 1,000 moves the point 1, 2, 3 places <b>right</b>; ÷ moves it <b>left</b>.</p><p>To <b>multiply</b> decimals, multiply as whole numbers, then give the answer as many decimal places as both numbers together: 1.2 × 0.3 = 0.36.</p><p>To <b>divide</b> by a decimal, multiply both numbers by the same power of 10 to make the divisor whole: 4.8 ÷ 0.6 = 48 ÷ 6 = 8.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Work out 2.5 × 1.4.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>25 × 14 = 350</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>2 decimal places</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 3.5</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Work out 7.2 ÷ 0.08.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>720 ÷ 8</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 90</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Moving the decimal point the wrong way when multiplying by 10.</li><li>Counting the decimal places of only one number.</li><li>Dividing by a decimal without changing both numbers.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "How do you multiply two decimals?",
   "a": "Multiply as whole numbers, then put in as many decimal places as the two numbers have altogether."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you divide by a decimal?",
   "a": "Multiply both numbers by 10, 100 … until the divisor is a whole number, then divide."
  },
  {
   "q": "Why does multiplying by 0.5 make a number smaller?",
   "a": "0.5 is a half, so you are finding half of the number."
  }
 ],
